PRO_0350,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"5. Manner in which complaint shall be made. - (1) A complaint in relation to any goods sold any service provided or agreed to be provided may b ***** (c) one or more consumers, where t interest, with the permission of the Dist consumers so interested; Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 by a civil court under Order I Rule 8 of the CPC. NCDRC allowed the application relying on the earlier decision Supreme Court in Chairman, Tamilnadu Housing Board v. T.N.Ganapathy20 and of the Full Bench of the learned NCDRC its in Ambrish Kumar Shukla v. Ferrous Infrastructure Pvt. Ltd.21. may note here, that Mr. Piyush Singh also placed extensive rel Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 23 Ambrish Kumar Shukla21.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0351,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"7. Before we get into an analysis of the rival c with specific reference to the statutory provisions, necessary to look into the reliefs prayed for, by th respondents in their consumer complaint and the plea the basis of which the reliefs were so sought. The r sought by the respondents in their consumer complain the benefit of and on behalf of the purchasers of al the entire residential complex reads as follows:-- ""That in view of the abovementioned facts an circumstances this Hon'ble Commission may graciously be pleased to pass orders and to OP to:-- i. Direct the OP to pay to each of the Complainants and to each buyer having same i delay compensation, as stipulated in the Sal Construction Agreements, for unpaid period o ""Total Period of Delay"" as indicated in Para Consumer Complaint; ii. Direct the OP to pay to each of the Complainants and to each buyer having same i compensatory interest @ 12% p.a. on individu consideration amount paid, for abnormal and inordinate delay in construction, till handi possession of flats to the complainants, com 20 1990 (1) SCC 608 21 2016 SCC OnLine NCDRC 1117 Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 period of delay as indicated in Para 46 of the Consumer Complaint; iii. Award cost of the Complaint t Complainants; and/or iv. Pass any other and/or further relief, which Hon'ble Commission thinks fit and proper, in the f and circumstances of the case, in favour of the complainants and against the OP."" Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 24",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0352,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. The pleadings on the basis of which the responden sought the aforesaid prayers, in brief, are as follows : the appellant launched the subject project in the year 2013; (ii) that the project styled as  Brigade Lakefront  to comprise of about 1100 units in three blocks, namely, Amber block, Blue block and Crimson block; (iii) that Amb block, also called Building No. 1, was to have seven wing namely, Wings A, B, C, D, E, F and G; Blue block, also called Building No. 2 was to have Wings H, I, J, K, L, M N and Crimson block, also called Building Nos. 3 and 4 we to have Wings O, P, Q, R, S and T; (iv) that in respect o flats in Blue block, the promised delivery date was 30.06.2016 with a six months grace period; (v) that thoug completion certificate and structural stability certifica also issued by the Consultant/Architect for the buildings Blue block on 3.05.2017, the occupancy certificate was is partially on 28.12.2018 and the occupancy certificate for balance was issued on 25.06.2019; (vi) that in respect of buildings in Crimson block, the promised delivery date wa 31.01.2018 with a grace period of six months; (vii) that though the completion certificate for the Crimson block w issued by the architect on 10.08.2018, the occupancy certificate was issued partially on 28.12.2018; (viii) th builder was guilty of unfair trade practice, inasmuch as terms and conditions of the agreement prescribed a paltry compensation of Rs. 5 per square feet to the purchasers, there was delay in completion of the project, while penal interest was levied on the buyers at 18% p.a. whenever th committed default or delay in making payment; (ix) that o account of the delay on the part of the appellant in hand over possession, the buyers suffered losses in the form o payment of monthly rent, interest on the loans taken and payment of higher registration charges, as the circle rat Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 gone up in the meantime; and (x) that therefore they wer constrained to file a complaint.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0353,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"9. From the aforesaid averments contained in the consumer complaint, it could be seen that the delay on th part of the builder in handing over possession, was the primary ground on which compensation was sought by the respondents. We have already extracted the prayers made i the original complaint. Interestingly the prayer portion complaint does not contain the quantification of the tota amount of compensation sought by the respondents either individually or collectively for and on behalf of all the purchasers of all the 1134 residential apartments. The pr portion of the complaint refers to paragraph 46 of the Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 25 complaint, for the purpose of computation of delay compensation. But paragraph 46 of the complaint does not convey any meaning except if taken into account along wit paragraph",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0354,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"5. Computation of ""Total Period of Delay""--The Complainants assert that the Total Period of Delay calculated as follows : Delay Period Start - Promis Date of Possession, not considering the grace perio and Delay Period End - Either of the following two dates based on facts of individual complainants: a. Where possession was taken prior to issuance Occupancy Certificate, the Date of Occupancy Certificate; OR b. Where possession was taken after the issuance of Occupancy Certificate, then Date of possession Offered; It would be relevant to state that the meaning and nature of  possession  as stated by the complainant this para would mean legal possession only where sa possession had been given or offered to be given up confirmation of readiness of the flat for possessio adherence to Schedule of Construction Agreement.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0355,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"6. It is clearly and unambiguously inferred that Buyers shall receive possession by executing the Sa Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 P Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 Deed and getting the same registered. Both actual possession and sale deed registration have to be d in unison in accordance with clauses of the agreem for construction. Hence, possession without regist and executing sale deed or vice versa does not tog construe to be ""possession"" for the purpose of calculating the delay suffered by the buyers. If b events are done on separate times, the later date two would prevail. It is respectfully submitted th the given residential project, the date of grant o Occupancy Certificate shall be reckoned as the piv event to ascertain delayed possession and calculat compensation based thereon.""",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0356,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"0. Paragraphs 45 and 46 contain a tacit admission tha period of delay in handing over possession of the flats, vary from buyer to buyer in respect of the purchasers of Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 26 the 1134 apartments. This is why the respondents have sou the indulgence of the Commission to compute the delay in respect of each case, on the basis of formulae indicated paragraph 45.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0357,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"1. However, paragraph 41 of the consumer complaint contains the valuation of the complaint, at least insofar 91 complainants who jointly filed the consumer complaint concerned. The relevant portion of paragraph 41 of the complaint reads as follows: ""It is submitted that as per the Agreement terms reproduced above, OPs are committed to pay meager delay compensation of Rs. 5/- per sq. ft. of salea area, per month, which comes to around 0.1% per annum of the sale consideration, or even lesser. O contrary, the penalty charged by the OPs in case t buyers' default or delay in paying the instalment 18% per annum. It is clear that the balance of performance is over 180 times against the buyers w have been bearing the brunt of the absolute mismanagement of project by the OPs. The buyer is only patiently waiting for the possession but also double whammy to keep paying all the instalments without enjoying the property. Of the total number complainants those who have preferred to approach this Hon'ble Forum in this instant Complaint, the Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 aggregate value of sale for 51 complainant-buyers alone, is about Rs. 66 Crore whereas the aggregate amount disbursed by the OP so the same buyers, in t name of Delay Compensation is a meager, less than Rs. 10 lakh which is just about 0.1% for the entire delay of more than 2 years.""",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0358,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"2. Before we proceed further we must record one important fact, namely, that even according to the respondents-complainants, the project comprised of three blocks namely Amber block, Blue block and Crimson block. Amber block was to have seven Wings with 386 apartments. It appears that none of the owners of these 386 apartment Amber block have joined with the respondents-complainants This is why the entire discussion about the delay in completion of the project, with reference to the timeline events found in paragraph 14 of the consumer complaint, refers only to Blue block and Crimson block. The appellan has given a tabulation in their counter to the original complaint, pointing out that Blue block comprises of 412 Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 27 apartments, out of which the owners of only 47 apartments have joined in the filing of the complaint and that Crims block has 336 apartments, out of which the owners of only apartments have joined in the complaint.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0359,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"3. In view of the fact that none of the owners of th apartments in Amber block have joined in the filing of th complaint, coupled with the fact that there is no pleadin respect to the timeline of the project in respect of Ambe block, the consumer complaint filed by the respondents cannot be treated as one representing the owners of 386 apartments in Amber block. The respondents ought to have either included as one of the complainants, the owner of of the apartments in Amber block or at least made necessa averments in the pleading about the timeline for completi the Amber block, to make the complaint, as one filed in a representative capacity on behalf of the owners of flats the three blocks. Let us now see at least whether the complaint was maintainable in a representative capacity o behalf of the owners of the flats in Blue block and Crims block, in the light of the requirements of Section 35(1)( the Act. Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 P Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0360,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"4. Section 35(1)(c) enables one or more consumers, where there are numerous consumers having the same interest, with the permission of the District Commission, file a complaint, on behalf of or for the benefit of all consumers so interested. It is needless to point out that sine qua non for invoking Section 35(1)(c) is that all consumers on whose behalf or for whose benefit the provis is invoked, should have the same interest. Interestingly, Section 35(1) (c) uses the disjunction  or  in between tw of words, namely, (i)  on behalf of ; and (ii)  for the b of . Clause (c) of Sub-Section (1) of Section 35 reads as under: ""one or more consumers, where there are numerous consumers having the same interest, with the permission of the District Commission, on behalf of for the benefit of, all consumers so interested.""",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0361,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"5. Therefore, a complaint filed under Section 35(1)(c) could either be  on behalf of  or  for the benefit of  al consumers having the same interest.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0362,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"6. Section 38(11) of the Consumer Protection Act, 201 Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 28 makes the provisions of Order I Rule 8 of the First Sched to the Civil Procedure Code, 1908 applicable to cases whe the complainant is a consumer referred to in Section 2(5) which defines a  complainant' to mean one or more consumers, where there are numerous consumers having the same interest.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0363,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"7. Order I Rule 8, CPC, unlike Section 35(1)(c) opera both ways and contains provisions for a two-way traffic. only permits plaintiffs to sue in a representative capaci also permits people to be sued and to be defended in an action, in a representative capacity. Order I Rule 8 read follows:-- """,PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0364,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. One person may sue or defend on behalf of all in same interest.--(1) Where there are numerous persons having the same interest in one suit,-- (a) one or more of such persons may, with the permission of the Court, sue or be sued, or may def Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 P Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 such suit, on behalf of, or for the benefit of, a so interested; (b) the Court may direct that one or more of su persons may sue or be sued, or may defend such sui on behalf of, or for the benefit of, all persons s interested. (2) The Court shall, in every case where a permission or direction is given under sub-rule (1 the plaintiff's expense, give notice of the instit the suit to all persons so interested, either by p service, or, where, by reason of the number of per or any other cause, such service is not reasonably practicable, by public advertisement, as the Court each case may direct. (3) Any person on whose behalf, or for whose benefit, a suit is instituted, or defended, under (1), may apply to the Court to be made a party to suit. (4) No part of the claim in any such suit shall abandoned under sub-rule (1), and no such suit sha be withdrawn under sub-rule (3), of rule 1 of Orde XXIII, and no agreement, compromise or satisfactio shall be recorded in any such suit under rule 3 of Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 29 Order, unless the Court has given, at the plaintif expense, notice to all persons so interested in th manner specified in sub-rule (2). (5) Where any person suing or defending in any such suit does not proceed with due diligence in t suit or defence, the Court may substitute in his p any other person having the same interest in the s (6) A decree passed in a suit under this rule sh be binding on all persons on whose behalf, or for whose benefit, the suit is instituted, or defended case may be.""",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0365,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. In simple terms, the salient features of the stipu contained in Order I Rule 8 CPC can be summed up as follows: Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 (i) where there are numerous persons having th same interest in one suit, one or more of such pers may, with the permission of the Court, sue on behal or for the benefit of all persons so interested; (ii) where there are numerous persons having the same interest in one suit, one or more of such pers may be sued or one or more such persons may defend such suit, on behalf of or for the benefit of all p so interested; (iii) the Court itself may, without the plaintiffs defendants seeking any permission under Order I Rul 8(1)(a), direct that one or more such persons may s or be sued or may defend the suit on behalf of and the benefit of all persons interested; (iv) notice of the institution of the suit to all p so interested either by personal service or by publ advertisement should be ordered by the Court in bot categories of cases, namely, where permission is gi by the Court on the application of the individuals direction is issued by the Court itself; (v) any person on whose behalf or for whose bene the suit is instituted or defended may seek to be m party to the suit; (vi) abandonment of the whole or part of the claim, withdrawal of the suit or the recording of any Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 30 agreement, compromise or satisfaction shall not be allowed by the Court unless notice to all persons interested in the matter is issued either by person service or by public advertisement. (vii) the Court may at any time substitute the pers suing or defending in a representative capacity, wi any other person, if the former was not prosecuting suit or defence with due diligence. (viii) the decree passed in the suit covered by thi Rule will be binding on all persons. Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 P Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0366,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"9. The Explanation under Order I Rule 8 is of significance. It distinguishes persons having the same in in one suit from persons having the same cause of action. establish sameness of interest, it is not necessary to es sameness of the cause of action.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0367,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"0. The Explanation under Order I Rule 8, is a necessary concomitant of the provisions of the Rules 1 an 3 of Order I. Order I Rule 1, CPC, allows many persons to join in one suit as plaintiffs. Order I, Rule 3 allows ma persons to be joined in one suit as defendants. But to fa under Order I Rule 1 or Order I Rule 3, the right to reli should arise out of or be in respect of the same act or transaction allegedly existing in such persons, jointly, severally or in the alternative. To some extent, Rules 1 3 of Order I are founded upon the sameness of the cause of action. This is why the Explanation under Order I Rule 8 distinguishes sameness of interest from the sameness of the cause of action.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0368,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"1. Since  sameness of interest  is the pre-requisite application under Order I Rule 8, CPC read with Section 35(1)(c) of the Consumer Protection Act, 2019, it was necessary for the respondents to include in the consumer complaint, sufficient averments that would show sameness interest. As we have pointed out earlier the total number residential apartments constructed in three blocks compri of about 20 wings (7 wings each in Amber and Blue blocks and 6 wings in Crimson block) were",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0369,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"4. There are no pleadings insofar as the purchasers of 386 residential apartments in the 7 wings of Amber block are concerned. Even in respect of the owners of the remaining 748 reside apartments in blue block and Crimson block, the complaint does not contain any specific averments regarding samenes of interest. The delay in handing over possession of the Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 31 residential apartments might have given rise to a cause o action for the individual purchasers of flats to sue the But sameness of the cause of action is not equal to samen of interest. The existence of sameness of interest, has b questioned by the appellant-builder on the ground that de compensation as stipulated in the Agreements was offered the purchasers and that some of them accepted the same without any demur or protest, while a few others have ref to accept. It is not clear from the consumer complaint as Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 how (i) those who have accepted the compensation under protest; (ii) those who accepted without protest; and (ii who refused to accept the compensation, have the sameness interest.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0370,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"2. The period of delay in the completion of the projec and the handing over of possession, does not appear to be uniform in all 1134 cases. The respondents-complainants cannot project sameness of interest for the purchasers in whose case the period of delay was negligible and those i whose cases there was a huge delay.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0371,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"3. We may have to look at the issue also from the po view of the buyers. The delay in handing over possession need not necessarily be the only deficiency in service on part of the appellant-builder. Some of the purchasers of may also have other complaints and their right to proceed against appellant cannot be stultified by a few individua invoking Section 35(1)(c). That a few purchasers have cho to approach the Karnataka State Consumer Disputes Redress Commission to ventilate their individual grievances shows that all the 1134 buyers do not have the same interest as of the respondents. At least if the respondents have give names of purchasers of all flats on whose behalf the pres complaint could be entertained, they would have been bett off. But they have not done so.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0372,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"4. Reliance is placed by the learned senior counsel f respondents, upon the Judgment of this Court in Chairman, Tamil Nadu Housing Board, Madras v. Ganapathy (supra), to drive home the point that the objec Order I Rule 8 is to facilitate the decision of questions which large number of persons are interested, without recourse to the ordinary procedure and that, therefore, t provision must receive an interpretation which will subse the object of its enactment. This Court pointed out in th case that though each of the allottees of plots by the Ho Board may be interested individually in fighting out the Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 32 demand separately made or likely to be made by the Board, would not make Order I Rule 8 inapplicable.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0373,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"5. But the above decision in Tamil Nadu Housing Board (supra) cannot be pressed into service by the respondents for two reasons, namely, (i) that what was Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 questioned in a representative suit in that case, additional demand sought to be made by the Housing on all the allottees uniformly, for an amount over the tentative price originally fixed; and (ii) that this Court restricted the applicability of the deci those allottees of the low income group. Therefore, sameness of interest has to be tested on the basis of the reliefs claimed and the pleadings that pinpo sameness of interest.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0374,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"6. Thereafter, relying on its earlier decision in Rame Shrivastava v. Dwarkadhis Projects Pvt. Ltd.22, Anjum Hussain Intellicity Business Park Pvt. Ltd.23 and Vikrant Singh Malik Supertech Ltd.24, the Supreme Court emphasised the fact that a common complaint could be filed by complainants who had  sameness of interest , vis-a-vis the alleged deficiency in se the service provider.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0375,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"7. The Supreme Court has, in this context, distinguish  sameness of interest  and  sameness of cause of action . In t before it, the Supreme Court noted that, though there were a n of purchasers of residential units in the project under consid before it, who had grievances against the builder, their griev were distinct and different. The judgment is also an authority proposition that  sameness of interest  should be manifest fro pleadings in the complaint filed before the Consumer Forum. In words, from the pleadings in the Complaint, the Consumer Forum should be in a position to hold that the persons whose cause t complainants before it were seeking to espouse had identical 22 (2019) 2 SCC 417 23 (2019) 6 SCC 519 Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 33 15:58:14 grievances against the opposite party and that the deficiency of the opposite party vis-a-vis the said persons were, from th pleadings, manifestly the same. Else, a consolidated con complaint as a class action would not be maintainable under Se 35(1)(c) of the 2019 Act - and, therefore, under Section 12(1) the 1986 Act.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0376,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. Mr. Piyush Singh has sought to submit that, in exam whether the complaint was maintainable as a class action petit Court was required to be guided by the prayers in the complain the prayers were identical, he submits that a class action com could be maintainable. He points out that, in the present case prayers in the complaint filed by his clients sought omnibus r complete the construction with all promised amenities and to h over vacant and peaceful possession of their respective flats occupancy and building completion certificate issued by the competent authority to the complainants as well as other allottees/purchasers/buyers within the stipulated time as may decided by  the learned NCDRC, failing which the petitioners o to be directed to give alternate flats of similar standards an area to each of the complainants, failing which they be direct compensate the purchasers and to refund the amounts paid by th Inasmuch as these prayers applied to all the allottees of resi units in the project, Mr. Piyush Singh submits that the learne NCDRC could not be said to have erred in permitting the compla be filed as a class action proceeding under Section 12(1)(c) o 24 (2020) 9 SCC 145 Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Pag Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 1986 Act.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0377,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"9. Ms. Agnihotri, learned Counsel for the petitioners submi contra, that the pleadings in the complaint itself indicate th no  sameness of interest  for all the allottees in the project she submits that no such sameness of interest is apparent even 51 complainants before the learned NCDRC. She submits that, in in respect of flat allottees, the scheduled date for taking of of the flats had itself not been reached, so that there could question of the allottees being aggrieved by any delay in hand the flats or by any deficiencies in the flats themselves. With the other allottees, as such, submits Ms. Agnihotri, even on f grievances of the allottees of the individual units in the com client were distinct and different, and no class action procee Section 12(1)(c) could be permitted to have been instituted. T Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 34 learned NCDRC has, in passing the impugned order dated 16 th M 2018, she submits, proceeded merely on the basis of the prayer complaint without examining the aspect of maintainability of t action proceeding as filed by the respondents with the serious deserves.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0378,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"0. Inasmuch as the number of allottees who have  sameness o interest  is not forthcoming or apparent from the pleadings in complaint, Ms. Agnihotri submits that it cannot be said that t of the allottees having sameness of interest, when consolidate exceed   1 crore, so that the question of the jurisdiction of NCDRC to entertain the complaint would also be highly disputab Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Pa Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0379,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"1. Having heard learned Counsel and perused the record and view of the law enunciated in Brigade Enterprises18, I am of t opinion that the submission of Ms. Agnihotri deserves to be ac and that of Mr. Piyush Singh, correspondingly, rejected.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0380,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"2. Brigade Enterprises18 is clear and categorical in requir in order to maintain a class action proceeding under Section 3 of the 2019 Act - or, correspondingly, under Section 12(1)(c) 1986 Act - the pleadings in the complaint had necessarily to unequivocally indicate  sameness of interest  of all the perso cause the complainants before the Consumer Forum were seeking espouse, vis- -vis the opposite party.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0381,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"3. In the present case, while there is an omnibus recital, of the complaint, that the facts relating to the allottees of are the same and that common issues disputes and controversies involved, with the allottees having common interest and having suffered identical deficiency of service, the pleadings that f thereafter belie this assertion. Para 16 of the complaint sets bulleted fashion, various alleged complaints relating to the a of units to the allottees in the project. The complaint doe however, identify these various perceived deficiencies in serv vis the allottees aggrieved thereby, by identifying the defici service by which the individual allottees were aggrieved. Nor any assertion, in the complaint, that each and all of the grie enumerated in para 16 of the complaint applied to every allott Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Pa Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 35 units in the complex, whose cause the complainant chose to es Even in respect of the 51 complainants before the learned NCDR the complaint does not set out, with clarity, their individual grievances, out of the several grievances enumerated in para 1 complaint. Rather, the use of the words  many complainants ,   complainants , and the like, which figure in para 16 of the Co indicate, prima facie, that the grievances of all allottees we identical, though, in the ultimate eventuate, their common aim have been to secure allotment to them, by the petitioners, of serviceable flats.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0382,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"4. Such a common ultimate aim cannot, however, connote  sameness of interest  within the meaning of Section 12(1)(c), Section 2(1)(b)(iv), of the 1986 Act. The law enunciated in Br Enterprises18 does not permit filing of a class action complai Section 12(1)(c) of the 1986 Act, in such a fashion. The matte merely one of the reliefs sought in the complaint. The 1986 Ac protection to consumers against deficiencies in service or per of unfair trade practices. The relief that follows is merely a The sameness of interest has to be with respect to the grievan complainants, and not with respect to the reliefs sought. Pare to brass tacks, hypothetically, if one allottee is aggrieved b leakage in the flat allotted to him, another by not allotment adequate parking space, a third by delayed allotment and a fou the flat not being of the category assured to him, they cannot a class action against the builder, by invoking Section 12(1)( on the ground that the ultimate relief sought by all of them i Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Pa Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 of flats as originally contracted. Once, as in the present ca para 16 of the Complaint), the complainants enumerated several individual items of grievance, the Complaint would either have assert that each grievance applied to each allottee whose caus were seeking to espouse, or to identify the allottees, grievan Else, the very requirement of  sameness of interest , in the c class action proceeding under the Consumer Protection Act, wou reduced to a redundancy, as, in every case, the consumers coul an omnibus prayer that the units should be allotted to them in condition and, on that basis, plead sameness of interest. This view, militates against the law laid down in Brigade Enterpris To reiterate what is required is that it must be apparent and forthcoming, from the complaint, that the consumers whose caus complaint seeks to espouse have sameness of interest, to the e that the deficiencies in the service provided by the service p qua each and all of the said complainants, is the same. requirement, in my considered opinion, is wanting in the compl filed by the respondents in the present case. Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 36",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0383,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"5. With greatest respect to the learned NCDRC, I am of the opinion that paras 5 to 7 of the impugned order dated 16 th Ma do not indicate that the learned NCDRC has approached the matt maintainability of the complaint as a class action in the mann envisaged by Brigade Enterprises",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0384,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. In fact, a reading of para indicates that the learned NCDRC appears to have proceeded on basis of the omnibus prayer clause contained in the complaint.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0385,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"6. The learned NCDRC has also noted that the complainants h Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Page Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14  same interest in the outcome of the complaint . Such an appr my considered and respectful opinion, cannot be accepted, in v the law laid down in Brigade Enterprises",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0386,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. What is required i sameness of interest in the outcome of the complaint, but same interest with respect to the grievances of the complainants an deficiencies in service that the complaints claimed to have su the instance of the opposite party. It is only then, that the Protection Forum could assess the correctness of the allegatio deficiency of service on the part of the opposite party vis-a- complainants. Unless the consumers who have sameness of intere respect of their grievances vis-a-vis the opposite party were immediately identifiable from the complaint, the complaint can maintained as a class action covering the interest of all such consumers.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0387,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"7. I am, therefore, unable to subscribe to the view expre learned NCDRC in paras 5 to 7 of the impugned order dated 16 t 2018.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0388,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. In my view, the complaint, as filed, does not indicate in respect of 51 complainants who were before the learned NCDR there is  sameness of interest  as could permit the complaint maintained as a class action covering their grievances vis-a-v petitioners. The pleadings in the compliant, are not suffic enable the Court to assess the number of complainants who woul have sameness of interest, in their grievances vis-a-vis the p It cannot, therefore, be said that the consolidated claim of s Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Pa Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 complainants who would have sameness of interest would be in of   1 crore. Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 37",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0389,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"0. Per consequence, the complaint filed by the petitioners applying the law laid down in Brigade Enterprises18, and on th of the pleadings contained in the complaint, be maintained as action in respect of all the allottees of the Indiabulls Green Project or even in respect of the 51 complainants who approach learned NCDRC.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0390,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"1. The complaint would, therefore, necessarily have to be dismissed, as, in the manner in which it is filed, it is not e that the complaint is maintainable before the learned NCDRC.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0391,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"2. Having said that, however, this order would not preclude complainants from filing a proper complaint, keeping in mind t observations contained hereinabove, as a class action proceedi under Section 12(1)(c) or otherwise, before the appropriate fo which might even be the learned NCDRC. Any such complaint, if when filed, would be decided by the concerned forum in accorda with law and keeping in view the observations contained in the judgment. Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 P Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 Conclusion",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0392,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"3. In view of the aforesaid, the petition is allowed. The i order of the learned NCDRC is quashed and set aside. The compl filed by the complainants before the learned NCDRC is also dismissed, reserving liberty as recorded in para 52 supra.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0393,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"4. There shall be no orders as to costs. C. HARI SHANKAR, APRIL 27, 2022 r.bararia/kr/dsn Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 38",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0394,Lucknow Development Authority vs MK Gupta,"Showing contexts for: consumer complaint deficiency service unfair trade in Lucknow Development Authority vs M.K. Gupta on 5 November, 1993 Context size: Smallest Smaller Small Current "" 'include' is very generally used in interpretation clauses in order to enlarge the meaning of the words or phrases occurring in the body of the statute, and when it is so used these words or phrases must be construed as comprehending, not only such things as they signify according to their natural, import, but also those things which the definition clause declares that they shall include."" It has been approved by this Court in Regional Director, Employees' State Insurance Corpn. v. High Land Coffee Works of P. F.X. Saldanha and Sons2; CIT v. Taj Mahal Hotel, Secunderabad3 and State of Bombay v. Hospital Mazdoor Sabha",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0395,Lucknow Development Authority vs MK Gupta,4. The provisions of the Act thus have to be construed in favour of the consumer to achieve the purpose of enactment as it is a social benefit oriented legislation. The primary duty of the court while construing the provisions of such an Act is to adopt a constructive approach subject to that it should not do violence to the language of the provisions and is not contrary to the attempted objective of the enactment.,PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0396,Lucknow Development Authority vs MK Gupta,"4. What is the meaning of the word 'service'? Does it extend to deficiency in the building of a house or flat? Can a complaint be filed under the Act against the statutory authority or a builder or contractor for any deficiency in respect of such property. The answer to all this shall depend on understanding of the word 'service"". The term has variety of meanings. It may mean any benefit or any act resulting in promoting interest or happiness. It may be contractual, professional, public, domestic, legal, statutory etc. The concept of service thus is very wide. How it should be understood and what it means depends on the context in which it has been used in an enactment. Clause",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0397,Lucknow Development Authority vs MK Gupta,"7. In Civil Appeal No. 2954 filed by a builder it was urged that inclusion of 'housing construction' in clause (o) and 'avail' in clause (d) in 1993 would indicate that the Act as it stood prior to the amendment did not apply to hiring of services in respect of housing construction. Learned counsel submitted that in absence of any expression making the amendment retrospective it should be held to be prospective as it is settled that any law including amendments which materially affect the vested rights or duties or obligations in respect of past transactions should remain untouched. Reliance was placed on Jose Da Costa v. Bascora Sadasiva Sinai Narcornim5; State of M.P. v. Rameshwar Rathod6 and Pulborough School Board Election case, Re",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0398,Lucknow Development Authority vs MK Gupta,"7. It was also argued that when definition of 'service' in Monopolies and Restrictive Trade Practices Act was amended in 1991 it was made retrospective. Therefore, in absence of use of similar expression in this Act it should be deemed to be prospective. True, the ordinance does not make the definition retrospective in operation. But it was not necessary. In fact it appears to have been added by way of abundant caution as housing construction being service was included even earlier. Apart from that what was the vested right of the contractor under the agreement to construct the defective house or to render deficient service? A legislation which is enacted to protect public interest from undesirable activities cannot be construed in such narrow manner as to frustrate its objective. Nor is there any merit in the submission that in absence of the 8/5/26, 2:00 PM Lucknow Development Authority vs M.K. Gupta on 5 November, 1993 https://indiankanoon.org/docfragment/1375046/?big=3&formInput=consumer+complaint+deficiency+service+unfair+trade 4/6 word 'avail of' in the definition of consumer' such activity could not be included in service. A perusal of the definition of 'service' as it stood prior to 1993 would indicate that the word 'facility' was already there. Therefore the legislature while amending the law in 1993 added the word in clause (d) to dispel any doubt that consumer in the Act would mean a person who not only hires but avails of any facility for consideration. It in fact indicates that these words were added more to clarify than to add something new.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0399,Lucknow Development Authority vs MK Gupta,"9. Facts in Civil Appeal No. 6237 of 1990 may now be adverted to as it is the only appeal in which the National Commission while exercising its appellate power under the Act not only affirmed the finding of State Commission directing the appellant to pay the value of deficiency in service but even directed to pay compensation for harassment and agony to the respondent. The Lucknow Development Authority with a view to ease the acute housing problem in the city of Lucknow undertook development of land and formed plots of different categories/sizes and constructed dwelling units for people belonging to different income groups. After the construction was complete the authority invited applications from persons desirous of purchasing plots or dwelling houses. The respondent applied on the prescribed form for registration for allotment of a flat in the category of Middle Income Group (MIG) in Gomti Nagar Scheme in Lucknow on cash down basis. Since the number of applicants was more, the authority decided to draw lots in which flat No. 11/75 in Vinay Khand-II was allotted to the respondent on April 26,",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0400,Lucknow Development Authority vs MK Gupta,"8. He deposited a sum of Rs 6132 on July 2, 1988 and a sum of Rs 1,09,975 on July 29,",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0401,Lucknow Development Authority vs MK Gupta,"8. Since the entire payment was made in July 1988 the flat was registered on August 18,",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0402,Lucknow Development Authority vs MK Gupta,"8. Thereafter the appellant by a letter dated August 23, 1988 directed its Executive Engineer-VII to hand over the possession of the flat to the respondent. This information was given to him on November 30, 1988, yet the flat was not delivered as the construction work was not complete. The respondent approached the authority but no steps were taken nor possession was handed over. Consequently he filed a 8/5/26, 2:00 PM Lucknow Development Authority vs M.K. Gupta on 5 November, 1993 https://indiankanoon.org/docfragment/1375046/?big=3&formInput=consumer+complaint+deficiency+service+unfair+trade 5/6 complaint before the District Forum that even after payment of entire amount in respect of cash down scheme the appellant was not handing over possession nor they were completing the formalities and the work was still incomplete. The State Commission by its order dated February 15, 1990 directed the appellant to pay 12% annual simple interest upon the deposit made by the respondent for the period January 1, 1989 to February 15,",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0403,Lucknow Development Authority vs MK Gupta,0. The appellant was further directed to hand over possession of the flat without delay after completing construction work up to June,PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0404,Lucknow Development Authority vs MK Gupta,"0. The Commission further directed that if it was not possible for the appellant to complete the construction then it should hand over possession of the flat to the respondent by April 5, 1990 after determining the deficiencies and the estimated cost of such deficient construction shall be refunded to the respondent latest by April 20,",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0405,Lucknow Development Authority vs MK Gupta,"0. The appellant instead of complying with the order approached the National Commission and raised the question of jurisdiction. It was overruled. And the appeal was dismissed. But the cross- appeal of the respondent was allowed and it was directed that since the architect of the appellant had estimated in October 1989 the cost of completing construction at Rs 44,615 the appellant shall pay the same to the respondent. The Commission further held that the action of the appellant amounted to harassment, mental torture and agony of the respondent, therefore, it directed the appellant to pay a sum of Rs 10,000 as compensation. 8/5/26, 2:00 PM Lucknow Development Authority vs M.K. Gupta on 5 November, 1993 https://indiankanoon.org/docfragment/1375046/?big=3&formInput=consumer+complaint+deficiency+service+unfair+trade 6/6",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0406,Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development,"Showing contexts for: consumer complaint deficiency service unfair trade in Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development on 7 March, 2022 Context size: Smallest Smaller Small Current On 4.5.2015, the opposite party sent the letter to the complainant of offer of possession for carrying out the fit out work in the flat alongwith the final statement of account, wherein the delay penalty was credited by the opposite party upto March, 2015 for a sum of Rs.6,19,875.00,when the completion certificate was applied with the concerned authority. Thereafter, vide mail dated 11.7.2015, the complainants raised certain objections to the offer for fit out letter and again asked the exact date of possession of the flat. It is submitted that the opposite party duly replied to the said mail vide their reply dated 12.8.2015.It is most humbly submitted that as on date the complainants have made a payment of Rs.34,18,559.00 towards the cost of the flat, car parking charges and free hold conversion charges.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0407,Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development,The opposite party as per flat buyer agreement has credited all delay compensations etc. to the complainants account. The construction in the tower in which the flat of the complainant is located has been completed and the completion certificate has been applied for by the opposite party so that the sale deeds of the flat may by executed in favour of the complainant. It is pertinent to state that the said project consist of 10 towers wherein the possession of the flats has already been offered in 7 towers wherein the buyers are residing peacefully.,PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0408,Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development,"It is submitted that in rest three towers bearing no.T-4, 5 and 6, the construction has been completed and few customers have taken the possession of the flat in these towers for carrying out the fit out work in their flats. The opposite parties have not at any point of time, violated or breached the provisions of the flat buyer agreement dated 7.11.2006, Hence, the captioned complaint has been filed without any cause of action. The complainants have not been able to establish any deficiency of service or consumer dispute as contemplated under the Consumer Protection Act, 1986 which could be attributable to the opposite parties.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0409,Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development,"Therefore, the complaint is liable to be dismissed in limine. No cause of action arisen in favour of the complainants and against the opposite parties for the purpose of filing the present complaint. In fact the complaint has been filed without any substantial proof.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0410,Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development,"It is submitted that bare allegation in the complaint without any proof and without any alleged act on behalf of the opposite parties 8/5/26, 2:02 PM Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development on 7 March, 2022 https://indiankanoon.org/docfragment/42621743/?big=3&formInput=consumer+complaint+deficiency+service+unfair+trade 1/5 necessary to charge it for unfair trade practice of deficiency of service, cannot be taken as gospel truth by this Hon'ble Commission to entertain the present complaint and hence, the complaint is liable to be dismissed under section 12(3) of the Consumer Protection Act, 1986. Now it is clear that there is deficiency of service on the part of the opposite party and also they are liable for unfair trade practice. For calculating the compensation/damages we are inclined that after two years the complainants are entitled to get the reliefs from the opposite parties.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0411,Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development,The cut-off date is May 2010. This is the date from which the amount of compensation/damages and other shall be calculated. The built up area of the concerned flat is 102.19 m .,PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0412,Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development,"Keeping in view all the above mentioned facts, we come to the following conclusions:- This is a case where the allottee has been given an allotment letter on 07.11.2006 and thereafter the complainant and opposite parties entered into an agreement for giving the possession of unit number T 5-1104. The allotment letter has specifically offered the delivery of possession of the unit within 36 months with grace period six months total 42 months meaning thereby that the said unit was to be handed over by May 2010. In this case the opposite parties issued many letters extending the time of delivery of possession but failed to deliver the possession within stipulated time.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0413,Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development,The opposite parties were showing daydreams to the complainant and other allottees but actually they were earning interest on the deposited amount of the allottees. A person who invests such a huge amount to get a flat for himself and his family must come under pressure and depression if not get the flat within time. The offer given by the opposite parties has been accepted by the complainant so now the opposite parties are bound by the promise and it is the duty to provide the flat within the time as specified by them.,PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0414,Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development,"This is a clear case of deficiency in service and unfair trade practice. Per Mr. Rajendra Singh, Member: This complaint has been filed by the complainant under section 17 of the Consumer Protection Act, 1986. 8/5/26, 2:02 PM Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development on 7 March, 2022 https://indiankanoon.org/docfragment/42621743/?big=3&formInput=consumer+complaint+deficiency+service+unfair+trade 2/5 This complaint has been filed by the complainant praying for a direction, to the opposite parties to rectify the inherent defects in the Flat No.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
PRO_0415,Veera Srivastava vs Parsvnath Development,"T5-701 having an area 202.06 square meters and to provide all the facilities/amenities addendum to the entire project, to direct the opposite parties to pay an interest @24% on the amount deposited by the complainant with the opposite parties from the respective dates of deposits till the date of possession, to direct the opposite parties to pay appropriate amount towards damages for committing serious deficiency in service and to pay appropriate amount towards compensation occasioned by the complainant, to direct the opposite parties to pay an amount of   20,000 per month towards rent payable with effect from June 2009 till the physical delivery of possession of fully furnished flat, to direct the opposite parties to pay the difference in the cost of escalation which shall be incurred at the time of the execution of the registered sale deed in view of the delay caused by the builder in delivery of possession, to award separate compensation and damages to the complainants for the loss and inconvenience caused to them by the deficiency in services coupled with unfair trade practice committed by the opposite parties, to direct the opposite parties to pay appropriate punitive/exemplary damages on account of mental agony, harassment and, underwent by the complainants and also taking into consideration the severity of the deficiency in service committed by the builder, to direct the opposite parties not to charge any service tax in pursuance of finance active, 2010 since the possession of the flat is to be provided to the ear 2009 and it is on account of the international and deliberate delay committed by the builder which is the reason for this payment of tax, to direct the opposite parties to pay a sum of   50,000 towards cost of the case and any other relief/order which the Hon'ble State Commission bus may deem fit and proper in the circumstances of the case. On 4.5.2015, the opposite party sent the letter to the complainant of offer of possession for carrying out the fit out work in the flat alongwith the final statement of account, wherein the delay penalty was credited by the opposite party upto March, 2015 for a sum of Rs.6,19,875.00,when the completion certificate was applied with the concerned authority. Thereafter, vide mail dated 11.7.2015, the complainants raised certain objections to the offer for fit out letter and again asked the exact date of possession of the flat.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,
