PRO_0983,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"7. Having heard learned Counsel and perused the aforesai decision, it is clear that Cicily Kallarackal8 addressed the i availability of the right to file a writ petition under Articl Constitution of India and was not rendered under the context o Article 227 of the Constitution of India. No doubt, t approach the writ court under Articles 226 and 227 of the Cons of India are, in a way of speaking, interlinked and cognate. T however, there is a subtle difference between the two provisio inasmuch as the jurisdiction exercised by a court under Articl the Constitution of India is in the nature of judicial review of, inter alia, the high prerogative writs of habeas corpus, c mandamus, prohibition and quo warranto, envisaged by the said Article, whereas the jurisdiction exercised under Article 227 Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 Constitution of India is supervisory in nature. Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 17",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0984,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. There is a fundamental jurisprudential difference betw judicial review jurisdiction and supervisory jurisdiction. The the power exercised by a court in each case is also essentiall different. The exercise of powers under Article 227, in a sens constricted than the exercise of powers under Article 226, ina the scope of examination of the merits of the decision under c is, under Article 226, more expansive than under Article 227. exercising jurisdiction under Article 227, what the court is e concerned with is ensuring that the courts and tribunals subje supervisory jurisdiction exercise their powers appropriately.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0985,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"9. The Article 227 court does not sit in appeal over the of the court or tribunal below. It is not expected to scrutini merits of the said decision with a view to correct the said de merits. If, however, the decision is one which involves errone exercise of jurisdiction or assumption of power where no power then the court, under Article 227, would be justified in inter Equally, if the decision demonstrates discharge, of the Court Tribunal below, of its functions otherwise than the manner in the law requires the Court of Tribunal to so discharge, the de be corrected in exercise of the supervisory jurisdiction veste High Court. Else, the position in law is trite, as enunciated Rubber v Dass Estate11, Garment Craft v. Prakash Chand Goel12 Puri Investment v. Young India13 that a writ court, under Arti 11 (2001) 8 SCC 97 12 2022 SCC OnLine SC 29 13 2022 SCC OnLine SC 283 Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Pa Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 is not even empowered to correct errors in the orders passed courts below. The distinction is as explicit as it is nuanced.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0986,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"0. Vishwabharathi House Building Coop. Society9 refers  Articles 226/227 of the Constitution of India . The opening s in para 53 of the report in the said case holds that adequate against orders passed by the learned NCDRC, are available to a aggrieved party by way of writ to a High Court under Article 2 or to the Supreme Court of India under Article 32 of the India way of an appeal under Section 23 of the 1986 Act. It does not however, provide any further guidance as to the circumstances which these remedies would, individually, be available against decision of the learned NCDRC. Even so, the view canvassed by Piyush Singh, if accepted, would amount to holding that, where appellate remedy under Section 23 of the 1986 Act is available Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 18 remedy under Article 227 of the Constitution of India stands irrevocably foreclosed, which would militate against the tenor view expressed in Vishwabharathi House Building Coop. Society9",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0987,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"1. The issue, however, stands largely answered, in pri the judgment of the Supreme Court in Virudhunagar Hindu Nadargal Dharma Paribalana Sabai v. Tuticorin Educational Society14 ( Virudhunagar , hereinafter), though the said decis not examine the issue in the backdrop of the 1986 Act. In that the Supreme Court was concerned with the aspect of availabilit remedy to the High Court, seeking exercise of its supervisory 14 (2019) 9 SCC 538 Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Pa Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 jurisdiction over the courts below, where a remedy of appeal the decision of courts below was available under the CPC. Para 13 of the report read thus:",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0988,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"1. Secondly, the High Court ought to have seen that a remedy of appeal under section 104 (1)(i) read with XLIII, Rule 1 (r) of the Code of Civil Procedure, 190 directly available, the respondents 1 and 2 ought to taken recourse to the same. It is true that the avail remedy of appeal may not always be a bar for the exer supervisory jurisdiction of the High Court. In A. Venkatasubbiah Naidu v. S. Chellappan & Ors15, this C held that ""though no hurdle can be put against the ex the Constitutional powers of the High Court, it is a recognized principle which gained judicial recognitio the High Court should direct the party to avail himse remedies before he resorts to a Constitutional remedy",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0989,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"2. But courts should always bear in mind a disti between (i) cases where such alternative remedy is av before Civil Courts in terms of the provisions of Cod procedure and (ii) cases where such alternative remed available under special enactments and/or statutory r the fora provided therein happen to be quasi judicial authorities and tribunals. In respect of cases fallin first category, which may involve suits and other pro before civil courts, the availability of an appellate terms of the provisions of CPC, may have to be constr near total bar. Otherwise, there is a danger that som challenge in a revision under Article 227, even a dec passed in a suit, on the same grounds on which the respondents 1 and 2 invoked the jurisdiction of the H Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 19 court. This is why, a 3 member Bench of this court, w overruling the decision in Surya Dev Rai vs. Ram Chan Rai16, pointed out in Radhey Shyam Vs. Chhabi Nath17  orders of civil court stand on different footing fro orders of authorities or Tribunals or courts other th judicial/civil courts . 15 (2000) 7 SCC 695 16 (2003) 6 SCC 675 17 (2015) 5 SCC 423 Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0990,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"3. Therefore wherever the proceedings are under the code of Civil Procedure and the forum is the Civil Court availability of a remedy under the CPC, will deter the H Court, not merely as a measure of self imposed restricti but as a matter of discipline and prudence, from exercis power of superintendence under the Constitution. Hence, High Court ought not to have entertained the revision un Article 227 especially in a case where a specific remedy appeal is provided under the Code of Civil Procedure its (Emphasis suppl",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0991,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"2. The afore-extracted passages from Virudhunagar14 disting between the situation where the order under challenge is passe civil court and a remedy of appeal lies to another civil court a situation in which the order under challenge is not passed b court, but by a quasi-judicial authority or tribunal, or where remedy of appeal does not lie to a civil court. In the former where it is a CPC-to-CPC appeal, the Supreme Court held that t remedy of appeal would operate as a  near total bar  to the av of supervisory jurisdiction under Article 227 of the Constitut India. Where, however, the order under challenge is not passed civil court, no appeal against the said order is available und or the appeal that is available is not to another civil court, under Article 227 does not appear to be foreclosed.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0992,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"3. In this context, the extracted words from the earlier de the Supreme Court in A. Venkatasubbiah Naidu15 are of some significance. In the said decision, the Supreme Court has held while there cannot be any hurdle to the exercise of constituti powers of the High Courts, where other alternate remedies are available, the High Court should direct the party to avail suc Signature Not Verified Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 20 Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 remedies  before he resorts to a constitutional remedy . Venkatasubbiah Naidu15, therefore, expressed the view that, be seeking recourse to the constitutional remedy of writ before t Court, other alternative remedies available to the litigant ou directed to be exhausted.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0993,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"4. This option is, obviously, not available where the other remedy is to the Supreme Court, as in the case of Section 23 o 1986 Act. The principle in A. Venkatasubbiah Naidu15, which ha been followed in Virudhunagar14 has no application, therefore, case where the remedy of appeal is to the Supreme Court, as un Section 23 of the 1986 Act, as there can be no question of the availing such remedy before resorting to a writ remedy availab the Constitution to the High Court. A party cannot be directed exhaust the alternate remedy available before the Supreme Cour before approaching the High Courts under Article",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0994,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"5. Relegating the party to the remedy of appeal under Secti of the 1986 Act would, therefore, operate to foreclose, once a all, the right to seek recourse to writ jurisdiction of the Hi under Articles 226/227 of the Constitution of India. This, in considered opinion, can never be the intent of the Supreme Cou Virudhunaga14 and would also militate against the opening sent in para 53 of the Vishwabharathi House Building Coop. Society9",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0995,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"6. For all these reasons, I am of the opinion that the righ petitioners, to approach this Court under Article 227 of the Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Pag Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 Constitution of India cannot be affected by the remedy of app Supreme Court available under Section 23 of the Consumer Prote Act.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0996,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"7. Having said that, it is also clear that any party which invoke the jurisdiction of the High Court under Article 227 su itself to the rigours of the provision and to the restrictions The High Court, under Article 227, cannot examine the matter w the same latitude as would be available to a Court which exerc appellate jurisdiction. Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 21",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0997,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. The contention of Mr Piyush Singh that Article 227 of t Constitution of India ought not to be invoked by the petitione remedy of appeal, against the impugned order of the learned NC lay to the Supreme Court under Section 23 of the 1986 Act, is fallacious on another count as well, which somewhat pre-empts discussion that is to follow hereinafter. Section 23 provides appeal against a decision of the NCDRC in exercise of the powe conferred on it by Section 21(a)(i). Section 21 deals jurisdiction of the learned NCDRC. Sub-section (a)(i) of Secti vests jurisdiction in the learned NCDRC to entertain complaint the value of the goods or services and compensation, if any, e 1 crore. The respondents have valued their complaint in excess crore on the premise that the complaint is maintainable as a c action proceeding on behalf of all the allottees of units in t As I proceed to hold hereinafter, however, the pleadings in th Complaint do not make out a case for justified institution of Complaint as a class action proceeding for all the allottees i Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 project, or even for the 51 complainants before the learned N Unless the number of consumers having ""sameness of interest"" i manifest from the pleadings in the Complaint, in the manner envisaged by law and as stipulated by the Supreme Court in Bri Enterprises Ltd. v. Anil Kumar Virmani18, it is not possible t that the cumulative value of the goods or services, or the compensation claimed, by all such consumers having sameness of interest, would exceed   1 crore. The very maintainability of Complaint before the learned NCDRC, under Section 21(a)(i) is, therefore, questionable. If Section 21(a)(i) is not applicable Section 23.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_0998,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"9. Where the justifiability of the invocation, by the the jurisdiction of the learned NCDRC under Section 21(a)(i) i in doubt, it is obviously not open to the respondents to cite availability of an alternate remedy to the petitioners under S as a ground to non-suit them under Article 227 of the Constitu India. A plea of alternate appellate remedy, predicated on err invocation of original jurisdiction of the Court or Tribunal b obviously has to fail.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
