PRO_1004,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"7. Before we get into an analysis of the rival c with specific reference to the statutory provisions, necessary to look into the reliefs prayed for, by th respondents in their consumer complaint and the plea the basis of which the reliefs were so sought. The r sought by the respondents in their consumer complain the benefit of and on behalf of the purchasers of al the entire residential complex reads as follows:-- ""That in view of the abovementioned facts an circumstances this Hon'ble Commission may graciously be pleased to pass orders and to OP to:-- i. Direct the OP to pay to each of the Complainants and to each buyer having same i delay compensation, as stipulated in the Sal Construction Agreements, for unpaid period o ""Total Period of Delay"" as indicated in Para Consumer Complaint; ii. Direct the OP to pay to each of the Complainants and to each buyer having same i compensatory interest @ 12% p.a. on individu consideration amount paid, for abnormal and inordinate delay in construction, till handi possession of flats to the complainants, com 20 1990 (1) SCC 608 21 2016 SCC OnLine NCDRC 1117 Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 period of delay as indicated in Para 46 of the Consumer Complaint; iii. Award cost of the Complaint t Complainants; and/or iv. Pass any other and/or further relief, which Hon'ble Commission thinks fit and proper, in the f and circumstances of the case, in favour of the complainants and against the OP."" Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 24",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1005,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. The pleadings on the basis of which the responden sought the aforesaid prayers, in brief, are as follows : the appellant launched the subject project in the year 2013; (ii) that the project styled as  Brigade Lakefront  to comprise of about 1100 units in three blocks, namely, Amber block, Blue block and Crimson block; (iii) that Amb block, also called Building No. 1, was to have seven wing namely, Wings A, B, C, D, E, F and G; Blue block, also called Building No. 2 was to have Wings H, I, J, K, L, M N and Crimson block, also called Building Nos. 3 and 4 we to have Wings O, P, Q, R, S and T; (iv) that in respect o flats in Blue block, the promised delivery date was 30.06.2016 with a six months grace period; (v) that thoug completion certificate and structural stability certifica also issued by the Consultant/Architect for the buildings Blue block on 3.05.2017, the occupancy certificate was is partially on 28.12.2018 and the occupancy certificate for balance was issued on 25.06.2019; (vi) that in respect of buildings in Crimson block, the promised delivery date wa 31.01.2018 with a grace period of six months; (vii) that though the completion certificate for the Crimson block w issued by the architect on 10.08.2018, the occupancy certificate was issued partially on 28.12.2018; (viii) th builder was guilty of unfair trade practice, inasmuch as terms and conditions of the agreement prescribed a paltry compensation of Rs. 5 per square feet to the purchasers, there was delay in completion of the project, while penal interest was levied on the buyers at 18% p.a. whenever th committed default or delay in making payment; (ix) that o account of the delay on the part of the appellant in hand over possession, the buyers suffered losses in the form o payment of monthly rent, interest on the loans taken and payment of higher registration charges, as the circle rat Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 gone up in the meantime; and (x) that therefore they wer constrained to file a complaint.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1006,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"9. From the aforesaid averments contained in the consumer complaint, it could be seen that the delay on th part of the builder in handing over possession, was the primary ground on which compensation was sought by the respondents. We have already extracted the prayers made i the original complaint. Interestingly the prayer portion complaint does not contain the quantification of the tota amount of compensation sought by the respondents either individually or collectively for and on behalf of all the purchasers of all the 1134 residential apartments. The pr portion of the complaint refers to paragraph 46 of the Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 25 complaint, for the purpose of computation of delay compensation. But paragraph 46 of the complaint does not convey any meaning except if taken into account along wit paragraph",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1007,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"5. Computation of ""Total Period of Delay""--The Complainants assert that the Total Period of Delay calculated as follows : Delay Period Start - Promis Date of Possession, not considering the grace perio and Delay Period End - Either of the following two dates based on facts of individual complainants: a. Where possession was taken prior to issuance Occupancy Certificate, the Date of Occupancy Certificate; OR b. Where possession was taken after the issuance of Occupancy Certificate, then Date of possession Offered; It would be relevant to state that the meaning and nature of  possession  as stated by the complainant this para would mean legal possession only where sa possession had been given or offered to be given up confirmation of readiness of the flat for possessio adherence to Schedule of Construction Agreement.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1008,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"6. It is clearly and unambiguously inferred that Buyers shall receive possession by executing the Sa Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 P Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 Deed and getting the same registered. Both actual possession and sale deed registration have to be d in unison in accordance with clauses of the agreem for construction. Hence, possession without regist and executing sale deed or vice versa does not tog construe to be ""possession"" for the purpose of calculating the delay suffered by the buyers. If b events are done on separate times, the later date two would prevail. It is respectfully submitted th the given residential project, the date of grant o Occupancy Certificate shall be reckoned as the piv event to ascertain delayed possession and calculat compensation based thereon.""",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1009,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"0. Paragraphs 45 and 46 contain a tacit admission tha period of delay in handing over possession of the flats, vary from buyer to buyer in respect of the purchasers of Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 26 the 1134 apartments. This is why the respondents have sou the indulgence of the Commission to compute the delay in respect of each case, on the basis of formulae indicated paragraph 45.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1010,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"1. However, paragraph 41 of the consumer complaint contains the valuation of the complaint, at least insofar 91 complainants who jointly filed the consumer complaint concerned. The relevant portion of paragraph 41 of the complaint reads as follows: ""It is submitted that as per the Agreement terms reproduced above, OPs are committed to pay meager delay compensation of Rs. 5/- per sq. ft. of salea area, per month, which comes to around 0.1% per annum of the sale consideration, or even lesser. O contrary, the penalty charged by the OPs in case t buyers' default or delay in paying the instalment 18% per annum. It is clear that the balance of performance is over 180 times against the buyers w have been bearing the brunt of the absolute mismanagement of project by the OPs. The buyer is only patiently waiting for the possession but also double whammy to keep paying all the instalments without enjoying the property. Of the total number complainants those who have preferred to approach this Hon'ble Forum in this instant Complaint, the Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 aggregate value of sale for 51 complainant-buyers alone, is about Rs. 66 Crore whereas the aggregate amount disbursed by the OP so the same buyers, in t name of Delay Compensation is a meager, less than Rs. 10 lakh which is just about 0.1% for the entire delay of more than 2 years.""",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1011,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"2. Before we proceed further we must record one important fact, namely, that even according to the respondents-complainants, the project comprised of three blocks namely Amber block, Blue block and Crimson block. Amber block was to have seven Wings with 386 apartments. It appears that none of the owners of these 386 apartment Amber block have joined with the respondents-complainants This is why the entire discussion about the delay in completion of the project, with reference to the timeline events found in paragraph 14 of the consumer complaint, refers only to Blue block and Crimson block. The appellan has given a tabulation in their counter to the original complaint, pointing out that Blue block comprises of 412 Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 27 apartments, out of which the owners of only 47 apartments have joined in the filing of the complaint and that Crims block has 336 apartments, out of which the owners of only apartments have joined in the complaint.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1012,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"3. In view of the fact that none of the owners of th apartments in Amber block have joined in the filing of th complaint, coupled with the fact that there is no pleadin respect to the timeline of the project in respect of Ambe block, the consumer complaint filed by the respondents cannot be treated as one representing the owners of 386 apartments in Amber block. The respondents ought to have either included as one of the complainants, the owner of of the apartments in Amber block or at least made necessa averments in the pleading about the timeline for completi the Amber block, to make the complaint, as one filed in a representative capacity on behalf of the owners of flats the three blocks. Let us now see at least whether the complaint was maintainable in a representative capacity o behalf of the owners of the flats in Blue block and Crims block, in the light of the requirements of Section 35(1)( the Act. Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 P Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1013,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"4. Section 35(1)(c) enables one or more consumers, where there are numerous consumers having the same interest, with the permission of the District Commission, file a complaint, on behalf of or for the benefit of all consumers so interested. It is needless to point out that sine qua non for invoking Section 35(1)(c) is that all consumers on whose behalf or for whose benefit the provis is invoked, should have the same interest. Interestingly, Section 35(1) (c) uses the disjunction  or  in between tw of words, namely, (i)  on behalf of ; and (ii)  for the b of . Clause (c) of Sub-Section (1) of Section 35 reads as under: ""one or more consumers, where there are numerous consumers having the same interest, with the permission of the District Commission, on behalf of for the benefit of, all consumers so interested.""",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1014,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"5. Therefore, a complaint filed under Section 35(1)(c) could either be  on behalf of  or  for the benefit of  al consumers having the same interest.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1015,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"6. Section 38(11) of the Consumer Protection Act, 201 Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 28 makes the provisions of Order I Rule 8 of the First Sched to the Civil Procedure Code, 1908 applicable to cases whe the complainant is a consumer referred to in Section 2(5) which defines a  complainant' to mean one or more consumers, where there are numerous consumers having the same interest.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1016,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"7. Order I Rule 8, CPC, unlike Section 35(1)(c) opera both ways and contains provisions for a two-way traffic. only permits plaintiffs to sue in a representative capaci also permits people to be sued and to be defended in an action, in a representative capacity. Order I Rule 8 read follows:-- """,PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1017,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. One person may sue or defend on behalf of all in same interest.--(1) Where there are numerous persons having the same interest in one suit,-- (a) one or more of such persons may, with the permission of the Court, sue or be sued, or may def Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 P Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 such suit, on behalf of, or for the benefit of, a so interested; (b) the Court may direct that one or more of su persons may sue or be sued, or may defend such sui on behalf of, or for the benefit of, all persons s interested. (2) The Court shall, in every case where a permission or direction is given under sub-rule (1 the plaintiff's expense, give notice of the instit the suit to all persons so interested, either by p service, or, where, by reason of the number of per or any other cause, such service is not reasonably practicable, by public advertisement, as the Court each case may direct. (3) Any person on whose behalf, or for whose benefit, a suit is instituted, or defended, under (1), may apply to the Court to be made a party to suit. (4) No part of the claim in any such suit shall abandoned under sub-rule (1), and no such suit sha be withdrawn under sub-rule (3), of rule 1 of Orde XXIII, and no agreement, compromise or satisfactio shall be recorded in any such suit under rule 3 of Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 29 Order, unless the Court has given, at the plaintif expense, notice to all persons so interested in th manner specified in sub-rule (2). (5) Where any person suing or defending in any such suit does not proceed with due diligence in t suit or defence, the Court may substitute in his p any other person having the same interest in the s (6) A decree passed in a suit under this rule sh be binding on all persons on whose behalf, or for whose benefit, the suit is instituted, or defended case may be.""",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1018,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. In simple terms, the salient features of the stipu contained in Order I Rule 8 CPC can be summed up as follows: Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 (i) where there are numerous persons having th same interest in one suit, one or more of such pers may, with the permission of the Court, sue on behal or for the benefit of all persons so interested; (ii) where there are numerous persons having the same interest in one suit, one or more of such pers may be sued or one or more such persons may defend such suit, on behalf of or for the benefit of all p so interested; (iii) the Court itself may, without the plaintiffs defendants seeking any permission under Order I Rul 8(1)(a), direct that one or more such persons may s or be sued or may defend the suit on behalf of and the benefit of all persons interested; (iv) notice of the institution of the suit to all p so interested either by personal service or by publ advertisement should be ordered by the Court in bot categories of cases, namely, where permission is gi by the Court on the application of the individuals direction is issued by the Court itself; (v) any person on whose behalf or for whose bene the suit is instituted or defended may seek to be m party to the suit; (vi) abandonment of the whole or part of the claim, withdrawal of the suit or the recording of any Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 30 agreement, compromise or satisfaction shall not be allowed by the Court unless notice to all persons interested in the matter is issued either by person service or by public advertisement. (vii) the Court may at any time substitute the pers suing or defending in a representative capacity, wi any other person, if the former was not prosecuting suit or defence with due diligence. (viii) the decree passed in the suit covered by thi Rule will be binding on all persons. Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 P Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1019,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"9. The Explanation under Order I Rule 8 is of significance. It distinguishes persons having the same in in one suit from persons having the same cause of action. establish sameness of interest, it is not necessary to es sameness of the cause of action.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1020,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"0. The Explanation under Order I Rule 8, is a necessary concomitant of the provisions of the Rules 1 an 3 of Order I. Order I Rule 1, CPC, allows many persons to join in one suit as plaintiffs. Order I, Rule 3 allows ma persons to be joined in one suit as defendants. But to fa under Order I Rule 1 or Order I Rule 3, the right to reli should arise out of or be in respect of the same act or transaction allegedly existing in such persons, jointly, severally or in the alternative. To some extent, Rules 1 3 of Order I are founded upon the sameness of the cause of action. This is why the Explanation under Order I Rule 8 distinguishes sameness of interest from the sameness of the cause of action.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1021,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"1. Since  sameness of interest  is the pre-requisite application under Order I Rule 8, CPC read with Section 35(1)(c) of the Consumer Protection Act, 2019, it was necessary for the respondents to include in the consumer complaint, sufficient averments that would show sameness interest. As we have pointed out earlier the total number residential apartments constructed in three blocks compri of about 20 wings (7 wings each in Amber and Blue blocks and 6 wings in Crimson block) were",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1022,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"4. There are no pleadings insofar as the purchasers of 386 residential apartments in the 7 wings of Amber block are concerned. Even in respect of the owners of the remaining 748 reside apartments in blue block and Crimson block, the complaint does not contain any specific averments regarding samenes of interest. The delay in handing over possession of the Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 31 residential apartments might have given rise to a cause o action for the individual purchasers of flats to sue the But sameness of the cause of action is not equal to samen of interest. The existence of sameness of interest, has b questioned by the appellant-builder on the ground that de compensation as stipulated in the Agreements was offered the purchasers and that some of them accepted the same without any demur or protest, while a few others have ref to accept. It is not clear from the consumer complaint as Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 how (i) those who have accepted the compensation under protest; (ii) those who accepted without protest; and (ii who refused to accept the compensation, have the sameness interest.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1023,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"2. The period of delay in the completion of the projec and the handing over of possession, does not appear to be uniform in all 1134 cases. The respondents-complainants cannot project sameness of interest for the purchasers in whose case the period of delay was negligible and those i whose cases there was a huge delay.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1024,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"3. We may have to look at the issue also from the po view of the buyers. The delay in handing over possession need not necessarily be the only deficiency in service on part of the appellant-builder. Some of the purchasers of may also have other complaints and their right to proceed against appellant cannot be stultified by a few individua invoking Section 35(1)(c). That a few purchasers have cho to approach the Karnataka State Consumer Disputes Redress Commission to ventilate their individual grievances shows that all the 1134 buyers do not have the same interest as of the respondents. At least if the respondents have give names of purchasers of all flats on whose behalf the pres complaint could be entertained, they would have been bett off. But they have not done so.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1025,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"4. Reliance is placed by the learned senior counsel f respondents, upon the Judgment of this Court in Chairman, Tamil Nadu Housing Board, Madras v. Ganapathy (supra), to drive home the point that the objec Order I Rule 8 is to facilitate the decision of questions which large number of persons are interested, without recourse to the ordinary procedure and that, therefore, t provision must receive an interpretation which will subse the object of its enactment. This Court pointed out in th case that though each of the allottees of plots by the Ho Board may be interested individually in fighting out the Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 32 demand separately made or likely to be made by the Board, would not make Order I Rule 8 inapplicable.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1026,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"5. But the above decision in Tamil Nadu Housing Board (supra) cannot be pressed into service by the respondents for two reasons, namely, (i) that what was Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 questioned in a representative suit in that case, additional demand sought to be made by the Housing on all the allottees uniformly, for an amount over the tentative price originally fixed; and (ii) that this Court restricted the applicability of the deci those allottees of the low income group. Therefore, sameness of interest has to be tested on the basis of the reliefs claimed and the pleadings that pinpo sameness of interest.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1027,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"6. Thereafter, relying on its earlier decision in Rame Shrivastava v. Dwarkadhis Projects Pvt. Ltd.22, Anjum Hussain Intellicity Business Park Pvt. Ltd.23 and Vikrant Singh Malik Supertech Ltd.24, the Supreme Court emphasised the fact that a common complaint could be filed by complainants who had  sameness of interest , vis-a-vis the alleged deficiency in se the service provider.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1028,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"7. The Supreme Court has, in this context, distinguish  sameness of interest  and  sameness of cause of action . In t before it, the Supreme Court noted that, though there were a n of purchasers of residential units in the project under consid before it, who had grievances against the builder, their griev were distinct and different. The judgment is also an authority proposition that  sameness of interest  should be manifest fro pleadings in the complaint filed before the Consumer Forum. In words, from the pleadings in the Complaint, the Consumer Forum should be in a position to hold that the persons whose cause t complainants before it were seeking to espouse had identical 22 (2019) 2 SCC 417 23 (2019) 6 SCC 519 Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Signing Date:02.05.2022 Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 33 15:58:14 grievances against the opposite party and that the deficiency of the opposite party vis-a-vis the said persons were, from th pleadings, manifestly the same. Else, a consolidated con complaint as a class action would not be maintainable under Se 35(1)(c) of the 2019 Act - and, therefore, under Section 12(1) the 1986 Act.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1029,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"8. Mr. Piyush Singh has sought to submit that, in exam whether the complaint was maintainable as a class action petit Court was required to be guided by the prayers in the complain the prayers were identical, he submits that a class action com could be maintainable. He points out that, in the present case prayers in the complaint filed by his clients sought omnibus r complete the construction with all promised amenities and to h over vacant and peaceful possession of their respective flats occupancy and building completion certificate issued by the competent authority to the complainants as well as other allottees/purchasers/buyers within the stipulated time as may decided by  the learned NCDRC, failing which the petitioners o to be directed to give alternate flats of similar standards an area to each of the complainants, failing which they be direct compensate the purchasers and to refund the amounts paid by th Inasmuch as these prayers applied to all the allottees of resi units in the project, Mr. Piyush Singh submits that the learne NCDRC could not be said to have erred in permitting the compla be filed as a class action proceeding under Section 12(1)(c) o 24 (2020) 9 SCC 145 Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Pag Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 1986 Act.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1030,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"9. Ms. Agnihotri, learned Counsel for the petitioners submi contra, that the pleadings in the complaint itself indicate th no  sameness of interest  for all the allottees in the project she submits that no such sameness of interest is apparent even 51 complainants before the learned NCDRC. She submits that, in in respect of flat allottees, the scheduled date for taking of of the flats had itself not been reached, so that there could question of the allottees being aggrieved by any delay in hand the flats or by any deficiencies in the flats themselves. With the other allottees, as such, submits Ms. Agnihotri, even on f grievances of the allottees of the individual units in the com client were distinct and different, and no class action procee Section 12(1)(c) could be permitted to have been instituted. T Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 34 learned NCDRC has, in passing the impugned order dated 16 th M 2018, she submits, proceeded merely on the basis of the prayer complaint without examining the aspect of maintainability of t action proceeding as filed by the respondents with the serious deserves.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1031,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"0. Inasmuch as the number of allottees who have  sameness o interest  is not forthcoming or apparent from the pleadings in complaint, Ms. Agnihotri submits that it cannot be said that t of the allottees having sameness of interest, when consolidate exceed   1 crore, so that the question of the jurisdiction of NCDRC to entertain the complaint would also be highly disputab Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Pa Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1032,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"1. Having heard learned Counsel and perused the record and view of the law enunciated in Brigade Enterprises18, I am of t opinion that the submission of Ms. Agnihotri deserves to be ac and that of Mr. Piyush Singh, correspondingly, rejected.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1033,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"2. Brigade Enterprises18 is clear and categorical in requir in order to maintain a class action proceeding under Section 3 of the 2019 Act - or, correspondingly, under Section 12(1)(c) 1986 Act - the pleadings in the complaint had necessarily to unequivocally indicate  sameness of interest  of all the perso cause the complainants before the Consumer Forum were seeking espouse, vis- -vis the opposite party.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1034,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"3. In the present case, while there is an omnibus recital, of the complaint, that the facts relating to the allottees of are the same and that common issues disputes and controversies involved, with the allottees having common interest and having suffered identical deficiency of service, the pleadings that f thereafter belie this assertion. Para 16 of the complaint sets bulleted fashion, various alleged complaints relating to the a of units to the allottees in the project. The complaint doe however, identify these various perceived deficiencies in serv vis the allottees aggrieved thereby, by identifying the defici service by which the individual allottees were aggrieved. Nor any assertion, in the complaint, that each and all of the grie enumerated in para 16 of the complaint applied to every allott Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Pa Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 35 units in the complex, whose cause the complainant chose to es Even in respect of the 51 complainants before the learned NCDR the complaint does not set out, with clarity, their individual grievances, out of the several grievances enumerated in para 1 complaint. Rather, the use of the words  many complainants ,   complainants , and the like, which figure in para 16 of the Co indicate, prima facie, that the grievances of all allottees we identical, though, in the ultimate eventuate, their common aim have been to secure allotment to them, by the petitioners, of serviceable flats.",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1035,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"4. Such a common ultimate aim cannot, however, connote  sameness of interest  within the meaning of Section 12(1)(c), Section 2(1)(b)(iv), of the 1986 Act. The law enunciated in Br Enterprises18 does not permit filing of a class action complai Section 12(1)(c) of the 1986 Act, in such a fashion. The matte merely one of the reliefs sought in the complaint. The 1986 Ac protection to consumers against deficiencies in service or per of unfair trade practices. The relief that follows is merely a The sameness of interest has to be with respect to the grievan complainants, and not with respect to the reliefs sought. Pare to brass tacks, hypothetically, if one allottee is aggrieved b leakage in the flat allotted to him, another by not allotment adequate parking space, a third by delayed allotment and a fou the flat not being of the category assured to him, they cannot a class action against the builder, by invoking Section 12(1)( on the ground that the ultimate relief sought by all of them i Signature Not Verified Digitally Signed By:SUNIL SINGH NEGI CM(M) 664/2018 Pa Signing Date:02.05.2022 15:58:14 of flats as originally contracted. Once, as in the present ca para 16 of the Complaint), the complainants enumerated several individual items of grievance, the Complaint would either have assert that each grievance applied to each allottee whose caus were seeking to espouse, or to identify the allottees, grievan Else, the very requirement of  sameness of interest , in the c class action proceeding under the Consumer Protection Act, wou reduced to a redundancy, as, in every case, the consumers coul an omnibus prayer that the units should be allotted to them in condition and, on that basis, plead sameness of interest. This view, militates against the law laid down in Brigade Enterpris To reiterate what is required is that it must be apparent and forthcoming, from the complaint, that the consumers whose caus complaint seeks to espouse have sameness of interest, to the e that the deficiencies in the service provided by the service p qua each and all of the said complainants, is the same. requirement, in my considered opinion, is wanting in the compl filed by the respondents in the present case. Lucina Land Development Ltd vs Union Of India & Ors on 27 April, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13785167/ 36",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
PRO_1036,Lucina Land Development vs Union of India,"5. With greatest respect to the learned NCDRC, I am of the opinion that paras 5 to 7 of the impugned order dated 16 th Ma do not indicate that the learned NCDRC has approached the matt maintainability of the complaint as a class action in the mann envisaged by Brigade Enterprises",PROPERTY,,,,,,,,
